hello and welcome to the cheeky science  show so in this video i'm going to focus  on  dietary protein restriction in  particular talking about a recent nature  aging publication  lifelong restriction of dietary  branched-chain amino acids has  sex-specific benefits  for frailty and lifespan in mice so to  break this video down i'll begin by  talking about protein restriction  and try and summarize the previous work  that has looked at the implications of  protein restriction  for health span and longevity and then  we'll talk about in particular why in  this study they focus on branch chain  amino acids what they are and  and why they might recapitulate the same  effects of just restricting protein  and then we'll jump into the results of  the study and look in particular  about these sex specific differences and  then lastly i'll give my views and  opinions and what further work i think  should be done  to further explore this topic but  firstly i just want to make a quick  disclaimer that i'm not trying to  suggest protein restriction here  you should consult your doctor before  trying anything the aim of this video is  to take a look at the data to have a bit  of a discussion  and it's not practical advice so firstly  then what is protein restriction  well you probably don't really need me  to explain that so i think the more  interesting question is why would  studies be looking at the implications  of protein restriction  and a lot of this in terms of longevity  research comes from the fact that  calorie restriction has been shown in a  variety of different model organisms  to extend not only lifespan but also  health span and calorie restriction here  is when  food intake is restricted by around 20  to 40 percent  but the interesting thing with these  observations is is it just a generic  reduction in calorie intake  or is it the reduction of specific  dietary macronutrients that's  the result of these improvements in  longevity and health span  now understanding diet is notoriously  complex and a lot of research is still  being conducted to try and  understand what particular components  are mediating the benefits seen in  calorie restriction  and so on from that a lot of research  has already been done to try and  understand  a reduction in protein and its  implication on longevity  one of the reasons for focusing on  protein is due to the fact that amino  acids that make up protein  activates the mammalian target of  rapamycin in its mtol  and activates this signaling pathway  which is notorious for  upregulating growth within a cell and  it's also known in studies that can  inhibit  the activity of this protein complex for  example using rapamycin have also been  shown to extend lifespan immortal  organisms  suggesting that part of the effects of  calorie restriction on improving  longevity  may be mediated through the reduction in  mtor signaling  mediated freedom reduction in protein  and one study that i want to bring up is  this 2014 cell metabolism paper  that emphasized that it's actually the  ratio of these macronutrients  not necessarily just calorie intake that  can dictate the health  benefits seen in calorie restriction and  they summarize their work quite nicely  in this figure here  where you can see that by the different  ratios of protein to carbohydrates  have different impacts for the metabolic  health and lifespan of the rodents  so as you can see in this figure here  low protein is associated with benefits  but these benefits seem to be enhanced  in terms of metabolic health and  lifespan when combined with high  carbohydrates  as opposed to high fats but we also know  from the studies already published that  it's not quite this clear  for example in this 2014 study they  showed that  the benefits of protein reduction may be  age dependent  for example they saw that respondents  aged 50 to 65  reporting a high protein intake had a 75  increase in overall mortality and a  four-fold increase in cancer death risk  during the following 18 years  however high protein intake in  respondents over 65  were associated with reduced cancer and  overall mortality  suggesting that low protein may be more  beneficial for those under 65  than those over 65 and more on from that  well we need protein  we need protein for muscle and it's also  been shown to reduce the risk of  sarcopenia moreover high protein has  commonly been assigned in diets  as it can make you feel more full but  all in all the evidence currently seems  to be swaying towards  low protein diets being healthier and  associated with longer life  and this reminds me of the moment in  down to earth with sac efron  when he was talking with falter longo  and was told that  low protein diets are associated with  longer lifespan  and i honestly really felt for zach at  that point he looked absolutely  terrified  but it is still yet more complex because  it's probably not only protein quantity  but also protein quality that is playing  a role for example there's been studies  that have looked at the differences  between  plant protein and animal protein with  plant protein generally being supported  as being healthier  and more from that as we'll see in this  study  it seems to be that certain amino acids  that make up protein  may be having a bigger role than others  and so there are 20 different  amino acids i once used to know how to  draw all of the structures out by heart  um i have to say i do struggle now with  the aromatic ones  and all of these amino acids vary in  terms of their so-called  r-group which give different amino acids  different biochemical properties  and one way of splitting up amino acids  are into  essential amino acids and non-essential  amino acids  there are nine non-essential amino acids  which means that our bodies can't  produce  these amino acids and that we have to  acquire them through foods  two of these essential amino acids  methionine and tryptophan  have been shown in separate studies to  extend lifespan in rodents  when these respective amino acids were  depleted  now this recent nature aging publication  instead decided to focus on  branched-chain amino acids that include  leucine isoleucine and phthaline  so all three of these are essential  amino acids that we get through diets  and of particular relevance to longevity  is the fact that  leucine is sensed by an inhibitor  of mtor activity and so to be a bit more  specific  leucine is sensed by the protein cestron  2  that would usually inhibit mtor  signaling so when leucine levels are  higher in the cell it binds to saturn 2  and inhibits mtor signaling so this  provided the rationale that potentially  reducing  the level of these branch chain amino  acids of particular relevance leucine  may be able to reduce signaling through  mtor which could subsequently have  beneficial impacts for longevity in  health span and another reason  for investigating this is to find  alternative potentially safer ways to  reducing mtor signaling  than just using rapamycin which is known  to have side effects  so what did they do in this study then  well the first thing that we should  really look at  are the different diets that they used  in the study so they decided to have a  control diet  where 21 of the diet is protein and then  for their low branch chain amino acid  diet  so again that's with leucine isoleucine  and phthaline  they restricted the 21 protein  by two-thirds and lastly they also had  a two-thirds reduction in protein that  produced all of the amino acids  so just a generic protein restricted  diet  and so importantly all of these three  different diets are isocaloric so they  all had the same amount of calories  and they had identical levels of dietary  fat  so what impacts did these different  diets have well firstly they tested  their different diets on a progeria  mouse model now we've briefly met this  model before when i spoke about base  editing and extending lifespan for these  progeria mouse models  and so they have a mutation in the lamin  a gene i won't go into the details  specifically here but effectively these  mice age more rapidly  and so what they found here is that by  giving them this branch chain amino  acids  restricted diets they improved their  lifespan in both males and females  so given this promising style they then  decided to test their reduced branched  chain amino acid diet  on aged mice so here they started the  diet with 16 month old male and female  mice  and in both the males and female mice  they found that those on the branched  chain amino acid reduced diet  weighed less than the controls and they  also were leaner  and they saw this effect even though  both the controls and the restricted  diet were eating the same amount  importantly they also saw in the males  and female mice with the  reduced branched chain amino acid diet  they had a lower blood  glucose level suggesting that these  middle-aged mice were more glucose  tolerant than the control mice  and another striking result was the fact  that the frailty score  which is a way that they can assess  health span of the mice were  significantly lower in the low  branch chain amino acids i'm gonna have  to find a final way of saying that  diet groups and both males and females  i'm gonna say low bcaa as you can see in  these two figures here  anyway despite the frailty index score  remaining lower in the low bcaa  mice the low bcaa diets didn't extend  lifespan in neither males nor females  as you can see here moreover they didn't  see a significant change in the levels  of  igf-1 which is a signaling molecule  upstream of mtor grave signaling  in either the males or females fed the  slow bcaa diet  however another interesting observation  is the fact that  whilst they observed cancer in 68 of the  control fed females  they only observed cancer in 33 percent  of the low bcaa  fed females there wasn't a significant  difference in the males  but this goes back to the understanding  that mtor signaling is  upregulated in cancer obviously due to  the fact that it can upregulate growth  and hence tumorigenesis  so would any of these effects be  enhanced if this low  vca fed diet was given through the  entirety of the mouse lifespan  well yeah by and large the results were  pretty similar  however this time there was no  statistically significant difference  between the control fed and low bcaa-fed  females  in their frailty index whilst in males  there was a significant difference  and correlating with this only in the  male mice  fed the slow bcaa diet did they see that  it significantly increased lifespan with  a 31.8 percent increase in median  lifespan  and quite strikingly one of the low bcaa  fed male mice actually lived to 1456  days  which exceeds the lifespan of the  longest live survivor in several  previous studies  of mice that are fed like ad libertarian  by approximately 25 now to understand  why there seemed to be these sex  dependent differences  they decided to do some transcriptional  profiling so effectively look at what  different genes are being  up or down regulated in these different  diets  in the different mice and quite  strikingly there wasn't really much  overlap between the male and female mice  on this low bcaa diet as you can see  here  i still understanding these differences  in terms of their genes  may be able to help elucidate why they  seem to be more beneficial for the males  than the females  and just a bit more detail in the males  they found that  many of the pathways that were  significantly upregulated  on this diet were centered around  metabolism and  longevity so again that gives us some  molecular underpinning as to how this  diet  is implicating in the improved health  span and lifespan  and you all know i do love a molecular  mechanism  so anyway further delving into this  transcriptional differences  and response to the low bcaa diet would  definitely help us better understand  what this diet is doing and how it's  acting but what else can we take from  this study  well it definitely supports the fact  that these different studies and  interventions  should be done on both genders as there  seems to be  sex-dependent differences and it also  reinforces the importance of diet and  diet to composition which i think we  kind of already knew  but reinforces the fact that it's not  just necessarily protein  quantity but also protein quality that  is playing a role  and in terms of these sex dependent  differences with longevity extension not  being seen in the female mice  it will be important to replicate this  data in different strains of mice with  different genetic backgrounds  more on from that it begs the question  of how translatable is this to humans  would it have the same impact and again  it's just so hard to say at the moment  given the data as clinical trials would  need to be conducted to investigate this  as well as try to better understand how  it may impact  both younger and older patients for  example given the fact that low protein  in over 65s may not be such a good thing  another thing i thought of whilst  making this video was given that i just  spoke about fasting mechanisms  was whether or not their strategy could  be more effective or more less effective  if it was done intermittently and so  it'd be interesting to test that  anyway so maybe in the future we'll be  loosening our leucine levels in our  diets  but we'll just have to wait and see so  with that i hope you've learned  something in this video  and a big thank you to all my patreon  supporters and  as always thanks for listening

